   227	  // create path below (`resolveEventAttributionsForItems`) already implements —
   228	  // see `primary` in connector-types.ts. Stopping at the first array hit made
   229	  // the answer depend on the order a connector pushed its identities, and
   230	  // `member_of` is a read ACL, so that was a mis-grant waiting to happen: a
   231	  // stale or recycled secondary claim (an old email, a reused login) would hand
   232	  // one person another person's channel access. A PRESENT primary identity —
   233	  // the source's stable per-account key — therefore governs alone and never
   234	  // falls through to a secondary match: a fresh primary means a distinct
   235	  // account even when a recycled secondary still points at the old person.
   236	  // This fast path only claims a member when its answer matches what the
   237	  // create-path engine would decide; every other case (primary present but
   238	  // unmatched, a same-tier conflict) falls through to `toCreate`, where that
   239	  // engine mints a new entity keyed on the primary or refuses the ambiguous
   240	  // match outright (no edge — fail closed) with its 'merge candidate' warning.
