195	Use `'cdp'` for services like Google that block headless browsers — it connects to the user's already-running Chrome session. Use `'cli'` for sites where attaching to a dedicated, user-signed-in Chrome (per auth profile) is acceptable.
196	
197	## Feeds
198	
199	Feeds define the data sources your connector can sync. Each feed has:
200	
201	```typescript
202	interface FeedDefinition {
203	  key: string;                    // Unique identifier within the connector
204	  name: string;                   // Display name
205	  description?: string;           // What this feed syncs
206	  displayNameTemplate?: string;   // Template using config values: "{repo_owner}/{repo_name} issues"
207	  configSchema?: object;          // JSON Schema for feed-specific configuration
208	  eventKinds?: Record<string, {   // Event types this feed produces
209	    description?: string;
210	    metadataSchema?: object;      // JSON Schema for event metadata
211	  }>;
212	}
213	```
214	
215	The feed key is passed to `sync()` as `ctx.feedKey`, so a single connector can handle multiple feed types by switching on `ctx.feedKey`.
216	
217	A feed's `eventKinds` are also the **default Behavior trigger catalog**. The first successful non-dry sync establishes a baseline without activation; later inserts whose kind matches a declared `eventKinds` key activate subscribers. A non-empty `behaviorEvents` declaration (camelCase in `ConnectorDefinition`, persisted as `behavior_events` in the catalog) replaces that derived catalog in the trigger picker. Keys that differ from the feed's `eventKinds` fire only when the emitted `EventEnvelope` carries matching `behavior_signals`.
218	
219	## Syncing Data
220	
221	The worker calls `sync()` for scheduled and manually triggered sync runs. It receives a `SyncContext` and returns a `SyncResult`.
